---
import Lecture from '../../../layouts/Lecture.astro';
import CodePlayground from '../../../components/CodePlayground.astro';
import Callout from '../../../components/Callout.astro';
---

<Lecture
  chapterSlug="11-debugging"
  lessonSlug="00-example-print-debug"
  hook="A function that runs fine but returns the wrong number. The hardest bug type — no exception, no traceback. Walk through finding it with print debugging."
>
  <h2>The bug</h2>
  <CodePlayground
    caption="sum_to_n(5) should return 15, but returns 10"
    code={`def sum_to_n_BUGGY(n):
    total = 0
    for i in range(1, n):       # bug: should be range(1, n + 1)
        total += i
    return total

print(sum_to_n_BUGGY(5))   # 10  ← wrong, expected 15`}
  />

  <h2>Step 1 — Add print scaffolding</h2>
  <CodePlayground
    autoRun
    code={`def sum_to_n_BUGGY(n):
    total = 0
    for i in range(1, n):
        print(f"DEBUG i={i}, adding to total={total}")
        total += i
    print(f"DEBUG final total={total}")
    return total

sum_to_n_BUGGY(5)`}
  />

  <Callout tone="tip" title="What the prints reveal">
    <p>
      The loop ran for <code>i = 1, 2, 3, 4</code> and stopped. <code>i = 5</code>
      never happened. Hypothesis confirmed: the range stops one short.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>Step 2 — Fix and verify</h2>
  <CodePlayground
    autoRun
    code={`def sum_to_n(n):
    total = 0
    for i in range(1, n + 1):       # FIXED
        total += i
    return total

print(sum_to_n(5))     # 15 ✓
print(sum_to_n(100))   # 5050 ✓`}
  />

  <Callout tone="note" title="From Think Python §6.2">
    <p>
      <em>"Once you get the function working, you should remove [the
      prints]. Code like that is called scaffolding because it is helpful
      for building the program but is not part of the final product."</em>
    </p>
  </Callout>
</Lecture>
